Hello,

We are looking for a scoped feasibility critique of an MIT-licensed, regtest-=
only Bitcoin consensus-extension experiment. It was developed with substantia=
l AI assistance and has not had independent architectural or cryptographic re=
view.

The design keeps the outpoint graph visible. Zero-valued native carrier UTXOs=
 commit to confidential claims, with separate fungible public reserve UTXOs b=
acking those claims. New validation enforces R=3DS, exact value bounds and au=
thorization. Transfers with public fee inputs avoid reserve contention; withd=
rawals and confidential-balance fees consume backing fragments and can still =
compete. Deposits/withdrawals and backing values remain public. The receiving=
 scheme is inspired by BIP352, not compatible with it, and carriers are exper=
imental future witness programs rather than ordinary Taproot outputs.

Our central question is whether the restriction to old-node-valid blocks and =
the backing invariant admit a credible upgrade, including historical-output, =
wrapped-witness, signature and reorg cases. Supply enforcement explicitly rel=
ies on commitment binding and proof soundness. There is no claim of preservin=
g plaintext supply auditability or of Bitcoin deployment readiness.

A separate secp256k1 aggregated-proof adapter matches baseline commitment byt=
es and retains MAX_MONEY complement checks. Two-output proof data measures 80=
3 bytes versus 16,664 baseline bytes, but it is not integrated into consensus=
. The historical library needed two documented scratch cleanup insertions aft=
er malformed-proof crashes. That patch and the cryptographic composition need=
 review. No node-throughput increase is claimed.

